<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ezeritai</h1><p class="st-meta">700 CE – 1300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Slavic tribe of the lower Eurotas valley in Laconia, a tribute-paying neighbour of the Melingoi under Byzantine rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Ezeritai, whose name probably comes from the Slavic word for lake, settled in the marshy plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helos">Helos</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> during the Slavic migrations into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> of the late 6th and 7th centuries. They first appear clearly in Byzantine sources of the 9th and 10th centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melingoi">Melingoi</a>, the Ezeritai kept their own leaders and paid a fixed tribute instead of regular taxes, recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> as 300 gold coins a year. They were subdued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theoktistos-bryennios">Theoktistos Bryennios</a> in the 840s and rebelled again in the 920s under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-i-lekapenos">Romanos I</a>, after which their tribute was raised. Little else is recorded of their society.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>They may have remained a distinct group into the period of Frankish rule in the 13th century. Over time they converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a> and merged into the Greek population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, leaving place names as their main trace.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ezeritai.jpg" alt="Ezeritai" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-fr.svg: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) derivative work: Constantine ✍ · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 CE – 1300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism, later Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Slavic, later Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ezerites</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1000&amp;focus=ezeritai" aria-label="Ezeritai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5206 761 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ezeritai lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5526 1017h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5526 1017h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greece. <a href="/atlas?year=1000&amp;focus=ezeritai">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

